MONOTONIC STACK – SUM OF SUBARRAY MINIMUMS

👉 Tests monotonic stack mastery
👉 Asked in Amazon, Google, Adobe, Flipkart
👉 Harder than Next Greater Element

1️⃣ Problem Statement

Given an integer array arr, find the sum of the minimum value of every subarray.

Since the answer can be large, return it modulo (10⁹ + 7).

Example
Input: arr = [3,1,2,4]
Output: 17

Explanation

Subarrays and minimums:

[3] → 3
[3,1] → 1
[3,1,2] → 1
[3,1,2,4] → 1
[1] → 1
[1,2] → 1
[1,2,4] → 1
[2] → 2
[2,4] → 2
[4] → 4
Sum = 17

2️⃣ Key Idea 💡 (CRUCIAL)

Instead of generating subarrays (O(n²)),
we count how many subarrays each element is the minimum of.

For each element:

contribution = arr[i] × left × right


Where:

left = number of elements greater than arr[i] on left

right = number of elements greater or equal on right

3️⃣ Algorithm (Monotonic Stack)
Step 1: Previous Smaller Element (PSE)

Use monotonic increasing stack

Find distance to previous smaller element

Step 2: Next Smaller Element (NSE)

Use monotonic stack again

Find distance to next smaller element

Step 3: Calculate Contribution

Add:

arr[i] × left[i] × right[i]

4️⃣ Java Code (Interview-Ready)
import java.util.*;

class SumOfSubarrayMinimums {

    public static int sumSubarrayMins(int[] arr) {

        int n = arr.length;
        int MOD = 1_000_000_007;

        int[] left = new int[n];
        int[] right = new int[n];

        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

        // Step 1: Previous Less Element
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && arr[stack.peek()] > arr[i]) {
                stack.pop();
            }

            left[i] = stack.isEmpty() ? (i + 1) : (i - stack.peek());
            stack.push(i);
        }

        stack.clear();

        // Step 2: Next Less or Equal Element
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && arr[stack.peek()] >= arr[i]) {
                stack.pop();
            }

            right[i] = stack.isEmpty() ? (n - i) : (stack.peek() - i);
            stack.push(i);
        }

        // Step 3: Contribution
        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            long contrib = (long) arr[i] * left[i] * right[i];
            sum = (sum + contrib) % MOD;
        }

        return (int) sum;
    }
}

5️⃣ Line-by-Line Explanation 🔍
🔹 Left Array
left[i] = i - stack.peek();


👉 Count of subarrays ending at i where arr[i] is minimum

🔹 Right Array
right[i] = stack.peek() - i;


👉 Count of subarrays starting at i

🔹 Why > on left and >= on right? 🤔

To avoid double counting duplicates.

6️⃣ Dry Run (arr = [3,1,2,4]) 🧠
